## 2. Projective operational-measure descent theorem

Let a unitary overlap `U_ij:H_i->H_j` transport a density and a two-outcome
projector context by

```text
rho_j=U_ij rho_i U_ij^dagger,
P_j=U_ij P_i U_ij^dagger,
Q_j=I-P_j.
```

Cyclicity of trace gives

```text
Tr(rho_j P_j)=Tr(rho_i P_i),
Tr(rho_j Q_j)=Tr(rho_i Q_i).
```

For the canonical recorder with common rate `gamma`, the stopped effects are

```text
F_ready(u)=exp(-gamma u) I,
F_a(ds)=gamma exp(-gamma s) P_a ds.
```

The output law

```text
mu_i(ready)=exp(-gamma u),
mu_i(ds,a)=gamma exp(-gamma s) Tr(rho_i P_a,i) ds
```

is therefore overlap-invariant. The selective instrument also intertwines:

```text
U_ij [P_a,i rho_i P_a,i] U_ij^dagger
  =P_a,j rho_j P_a,j.
```

If a cycle closes by a scalar phase, the density, effects, instrument and
probabilities close exactly because conjugation cancels that phase. Thus the
canonical recorder is naturally ray/density valued; it does not require an
ordinary globally single-valued wavefunction representative.

